format 1
adapter curriculum 1
unit: radioactivity

objective u2-not-always-dangerous

statement:
    Know that radioactive does not automatically mean dangerous; it depends on the type, the amount and how it reaches the body.

misconception: Radioactive means automatically dangerous.

item u2-l5-choice-dangerous
type: choice
surfaces: lesson
surfaces: practice
surfaces: review
references: ref-epa-radiation-sources

prompt:
    If something is radioactive, is it automatically dangerous?

options:
    {"id":"not","label":"Not necessarily"}
    {"id":"always","label":"Yes: anything radioactive is dangerous","note":"Bananas and your body are radioactive. It depends on the type, the amount and how it reaches the body."}
    {"id":"never","label":"No: radioactive things are always harmless","note":"Some radioactive things can be harmful. It depends."}

answer: not

explanation:
    Radioactive does not automatically mean dangerous. It depends on the type of radiation, the amount and how it reaches the body.

item u2-l5-choice-decides
type: choice
difficulty: 2
surfaces: lesson
surfaces: practice
surfaces: review
references: ref-epa-radiation-sources

prompt:
    What helps decide whether radiation matters?

options:
    {"id":"three","label":"The type, the amount and how it reaches the body"}
    {"id":"only-radioactive","label":"Only whether the material is radioactive","note":"That is not enough. It depends on more than that."}
    {"id":"only-old","label":"Only how old the material is","note":"Age alone does not decide it."}

answer: three

explanation:
    The type of radiation, how much there is and how it reaches the body all matter. Radioactive alone does not tell you.

item u2-x-banana-says
type: choice
surfaces: practice
surfaces: checkpoint
surfaces: review
references: ref-epa-radiation-sources

prompt:
    A banana holds a little radioactive potassium. What does that tell you?

options:
    {"id":"not","label":"Radioactive does not automatically mean dangerous"}
    {"id":"hazard","label":"Bananas are a nuclear hazard","note":"A tiny natural amount is normal."}
    {"id":"made","label":"Bananas were made by people","note":"Potassium-40 is natural."}

answer: not

explanation:
    Bananas are a little radioactive, and that is normal. Radioactive does not automatically mean dangerous.

item u2-x-tom-dangerous
type: true_or_myth
surfaces: practice
surfaces: checkpoint
surfaces: review
references: ref-epa-radiation-sources

statement:
    Anything radioactive is automatically dangerous.

answer: myth

explanation:
    Myth. It depends on the type of radiation, the amount and how it reaches the body.
